# Engineering

Details: `docs/conventions.md`. C# style: `.cursor/rules/csharp.mdc`. Tests: `.cursor/rules/testing.mdc`.

## Layout

- Solution: `XylemUI.slnx` only. Do not commit a `.sln`.
- Projects follow `docs/design.ja.md` §2.9.1: `src/XylemUI.*`, `tests/*.Tests`, `samples/`, `benchmarks/`, `tools/`, `vendor/`. Do not collapse them into one project, and do not create a project, folder, script, or skill step before the Issue that needs it.
- `XylemUI.Core` references no `XylemUI.Platform.*` or `XylemUI.Rendering.*` project (Invariants 9, 10). The dependency direction is `XylemUI.Core` ← `XylemUI.Primitives` ← `XylemUI.Theme.Fluent`.
- A project's line in `docs/conventions.md` is added in the PR that adds the project.

## Targets and build

- `net10.0` everywhere (`Directory.Build.props`). C# 14. Nullable on. `InvariantGlobalization` on.
- `TreatWarningsAsErrors` is on from the first commit. Do not add `NoWarn` or `#pragma warning disable` to get a build through; fix the cause, or change `.editorconfig` with the reason in a comment when a rule is wrong for this repository, in a PR that says so.
- Before a stricter rule is turned on, what it will flag is counted on the whole solution with `-p:TreatWarningsAsErrors=false`, then the warnings of that rule are counted without repeats. With warnings as errors the build stops at the first project that fails, and the projects that depend on it are not built: in Q6 the first count was 73 (`XylemUI.Text` only) and the whole was 605 (PR #450). The setting is for that count only, never for a commit.
- Libraries under `src/` are `IsAotCompatible` (`.cursor/rules/aot.mdc`) and need XML docs on the public API.
- When NuGet's audit fails the build on an advisory for a pack the SDK brings (`Microsoft.DotNet.ILCompiler`), the fix is the patched SDK. Install it without UAC with Microsoft's `dotnet-install.ps1` into `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Microsoft\dotnet`, use it for the session through `DOTNET_ROOT` and `PATH`, ask the owner to update the machine's SDK, and remove the local one after. CI takes the latest patch through `global.json` (GHSA-p3jp-9v83-76w9, PR #634).
- A generated file that names its source's version or commit reads it from where the source is pinned (`vendor/versions.lock`), not from a string in the generator: IC1's generator wrote "1.1.344" by hand, which a raise of the pin would have left stale (PR #726's review).
- Package versions live in `Directory.Packages.props` (Central Package Management). A new package goes through `.cursor/rules/dependencies.mdc` first.
- Engine projects (everything under `src/` except `XylemUI.Platform.*` and `XylemUI.Testing`) build with `BannedSymbols.txt`. Do not remove an entry to get a build through.

## Format and analyzers

- CSharpier is the only formatter (`.config/dotnet-tools.json`, `.csharpierrc.json`). It also formats `.csproj` and `.props`. Run `dotnet csharpier format .` before a commit.
- StyleCop.Analyzers and the .NET analyzers at `latest-recommended` run in every build. `EnforceCodeStyleInBuild` is on.
- Do not use an alignment in an interpolated string (`{x,5}`): CSharpier writes it `{x, 5}`, which StyleCop's SA1001 refuses. Pad with `PadLeft` instead (T1a, PR #285).

A file found from the caller's source path is found another way: under `ContinuousIntegrationBuild` with `Deterministic` (`Directory.Build.props`), the SDK maps source paths to `/_/`, so in CI `[CallerFilePath]` names no file on disk. Set the path from MSBuild instead (an `AssemblyMetadata` attribute); P02a2's design first found the goldens with `[CallerFilePath]`, and the review caught it (PR #465).

## Gate

Before a push, `./build.ps1 -Affected -Parallel`: the same steps, with only the test projects that the branch's changes reach, run at the same time. A test project is reached when a changed file is in its folder or in the folder of a project it references, directly or through others; a change outside the projects' folders (the build's settings, `vendor/`, the script itself) or in `src/XylemUI.Generators`, which every project runs as an analyzer, reaches them all. The changes are those of the branch since `origin/main` and of the working tree. CI runs `./build.ps1` with every test project on Windows, Linux, and macOS, and a PR is merged after it passed: a break in a project the change was not thought to reach shows there, 15 to 25 minutes later. The whole gate took 11.6 minutes on the owner's machine with the native libraries up to date, 370 seconds of it in `XylemUI.Rendering.D3D11.Tests`, and a row ran it seven times (Issue #822).

A change to `XylemUI.Core` or `XylemUI.Text` reaches nine of the ten test projects, since they all reference them, so `-Affected` saves time only for a row that stays in a package above them (`XylemUI.Primitives`: three projects) or in a sample or a test. `-Parallel` is what shortens the others: the ten projects of Release took 187 seconds at once and about 305 one after the other, and the whole gate with every project reached took 428 seconds in place of 697, all passing. A test that fails under `-Parallel` is run again alone before it is called a failure: the tests that time the app's loop are known to fail under load (Issue #793). CI runs the projects one after the other, and that run is the reference.

`./build.ps1` with no switch runs everything, one project after the other, as before. It runs tool restore, the CSharpier check, the native libraries (`vendor/build.ps1`: HarfBuzz from the source pinned in `vendor/versions.lock`, with CMake and MSVC; it does nothing when the library is up to date), restore, then for Release and for Debug a build and every test project. Release is what ships; Debug runs the checks that only exist there (`Invariant.Fail` throws instead of ending the process). Hosted CI runs the same script.

A new git worktree has no `vendor/out` (it is build output, gitignored), so a NativeAOT link there cannot open `harfbuzz.lib` (`LNK1181`, L1a). Run `vendor/build.ps1` in it, or copy `vendor/out` from the main checkout for a scratch comparison. The main checkout's `vendor/out` can be older than `vendor/versions.lock`: in P02f's second implementation PR it had no `accesskit.lib`, which P02g added, and a NativeAOT publish in the worktree that copied it stopped with `LNK1181`. Copy it only when its libraries are those `versions.lock` names; otherwise run `vendor/build.ps1` (PR #658). The tests that shape text fail there too, with `DllNotFoundException` for `harfbuzz`; P02d's second PR ran them in a new worktree before `vendor/build.ps1` (PR #556). A `vendor/out` copied from another worktree comes from a tree whose `native/` and `vendor/` are at the same commit: the shim's functions are called through P/Invoke, and a function whose arguments changed (`xi_info` in IM1 3c) crashes the process instead of failing a test (PR #787's retrospective).

A script calls an operating-system tool by its full path, not the first one on `PATH`: run from Git Bash, `tar` is GNU tar, which reads the `C:` of a path as a remote host (S8a; `vendor/build.ps1` calls `System32/tar.exe`).

A commit and a push follow the build only through its exit code. Piping the build's output into `grep` and chaining `git commit ... && git push` after it runs the commit even when the build failed: in S6a a commit that did not compile reached the PR that way. Write `if dotnet build ...; then git commit ...; fi`, or check `$LASTEXITCODE` in PowerShell.

If `dotnet --list-sdks` does not show a 10.0 SDK, install it; do not stop to ask.

A NativeAOT publish on Windows needs `C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft Visual Studio\Installer` on `PATH`: Visual Studio's environment scripts call `vswhere.exe` by name, and when it is missing its error text ends up in the linker command (`MSB3073 ... 'vswhere.exe' is not recognized`). Add it for the session (`$env:PATH += ';C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft Visual Studio\Installer'`) before `dotnet publish`; from Bash, add `/c/Program Files (x86)/Microsoft Visual Studio/Installer` to `PATH` the same way (in S8b a publish from Bash failed with this error). If `dotnet csharpier` says to run `dotnet tool restore` right after a successful restore, delete `%USERPROFILE%\.dotnet\toolResolverCache\1\csharpier` (a stale cache entry from another sandbox) and restore again.
